{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan review: PASS (0 blocking, 4 advisory), model bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6.\nDispositions for all four advisories:\n\nE1 + E3 (apply-now, priority 1.0) — \"AC1 omits ready_tickets although the Impact section names\nit as one of the silently-empty reads\". ACCEPTED AND FIXED. This was a real under-coverage: the\nImpact section lists `list_tickets / search / ready_tickets -> []` but AC1 enumerated only\nlist_tickets, search, recent_session_logs. AC1 is amended to read:\n\n  AC1: A library read — `list_tickets`, `search`, `ready`, `recent_session_logs` (the MCP tool\n  spelling of `ready` is `ready_tickets`) — against a repo with no tracker directory raises the\n  same not-initialized RebarError the write path raises, instead of returning [].\n\nThe regression test is extended to exercise `rebar.ready` alongside the other three. Because the\nfix already existed in the tree when this gap was found, the added coverage was NOT simply\nappended to a passing suite: the fix was reverted first, the strengthened test was re-verified\nRED with no fix present, and only then was the fix re-applied — the test-modification ordering\nthe debug protocol requires.\n\nE2 (deferred, priority 0.17) — \"state the fix locus explicitly rather than leaving it inferable\".\nACCEPTED as documentation. The locus is now stated in the code itself: the guard lives at the\nsingle `_reads._tracker()` funnel point, so all seven read entries inherit it uniformly; the ACs\nsample the user-visible ones rather than enumerating internals.\n\nG6 (deferred, priority 0.52) — \"the two cited parity targets use DIFFERENT guard predicates; the\nplan does not say which the new guard uses\". ACCEPTED as a real ambiguity, and resolved\ndeliberately AGAINST the reviewer's suggested fix. The facts are correct:\n\n  write path    event_prepare.py:101   isdir(tracker) AND exists(tracker/.git)\n  CLI read path reads_cli.py:340       isdir(tracker) only\n\nThe reviewer suggested adopting the stronger write-path predicate \"for true read/write parity\".\nWe deliberately chose `isdir` only, matching the CLI READ path, because:\n\n1. The surface being brought into parity is the READ surface. The defect was that library reads\n   disagreed with CLI reads; matching `reads_cli.py:340` closes exactly that gap. Adopting the\n   write predicate would make library reads STRICTER than CLI reads and open a NEW divergence in\n   the opposite direction — trading one asymmetry for another.\n2. A tracker directory that exists WITHOUT `.git` is a real, supported read case in this repo,\n   not a hypothetical. `tests/unit/test_store_copy_needs_env_id.py` documents a store\n   materialised by `git archive` of the orphan `tickets` branch: writes are refused on it while\n   reads are expected to work. A `.git`-requiring read guard would break that fixture and the\n   J11 live-DC cells that depend on it.\n3. The write path's extra `.git` check exists to protect event APPENDS (provenance stamping),\n   which has no read-side analogue.\n\nSo the divergence between the two predicates is intentional and load-bearing, not an oversight —\nreads and writes legitimately answer different questions about the same directory. Recorded here\nrather than as an ADR (the E2 coaching move suggested one) because this is a restatement of\nexisting behavior, not a one-way door."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787706559561878001,"uuid":"40789ef1-1b14-4f75-96e0-8b5bf99b4330"}